WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.400
How can a bat wing and a protein point to the same relatives?

2
00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:07.580
The first film used fossils to track change through time.

3
00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:11.380
Now we will test relationships among the branches.

4
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.960
A common ancestor is an earlier population from which later groups came.

5
00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:22.620
Picture one branch splitting.

6
00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:28.200
Each new branch inherits old features, then changes in its own direction.

7
00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:34.430
A human arm, cat leg, whale flipper, and bat wing have an upper bone and paired

8
00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:37.950
lower bones. Wrist and finger bones follow.

9
00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:41.750
Shapes and jobs differ, but positions match.

10
00:00:42.833 --> 00:00:46.833
Scientists compare construction and position, not only jobs.

11
00:00:48.393 --> 00:00:51.213
Inherited changes can reshape an old set of parts.

12
00:00:52.373 --> 00:00:55.393
That shared bone map points back toward a common ancestor.

13
00:00:56.533 --> 00:00:58.353
Sequences offer a test.

14
00:00:59.593 --> 00:01:02.073
A DNA sequence is the order of its bases.

15
00:01:03.573 --> 00:01:05.993
Many genes carry instructions for proteins.

16
00:01:07.513 --> 00:01:12.233
Scientists can line up the same protein from several species, position by position.

17
00:01:13.383 --> 00:01:17.963
Some inherited DNA changes remain in later populations.

18
00:01:19.443 --> 00:01:24.223
Branches split at different times, so closer relatives usually have more similar

19
00:01:24.223 --> 00:01:27.083
sequences. Usually matters.

20
00:01:28.583 --> 00:01:30.803
Changes do not build up at one steady rate.

21
00:01:31.833 --> 00:01:33.313
Now test two clues.

22
00:01:34.933 --> 00:01:37.253
Species A and B share a blue structure that C lacks.

23
00:01:38.653 --> 00:01:44.093
A teal protein strip joins A with C. Have the clues agreed, and should any branch

24
00:01:44.093 --> 00:01:45.093
turn green yet?

25
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:54.880
No. The clues support different groupings.

26
00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:59.920
Keep the branches gray, check the comparison, and collect more evidence.

27
00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.180
A conflict is not something to hide.

28
00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:05.980
It is a question to investigate.

29
00:02:07.083 --> 00:02:08.423
Now a different case.

30
00:02:09.703 --> 00:02:12.823
Two species share a structure that a third species lacks.

31
00:02:14.063 --> 00:02:16.123
The protein strip groups that same pair.

32
00:02:17.383 --> 00:02:21.623
When independent clues agree, their shared branch is the stronger explanation.

33
00:02:22.933 --> 00:02:25.573
This is how a scientific theory earns trust.

34
00:02:26.853 --> 00:02:29.473
Each line of evidence could have told a different story.

35
00:02:30.793 --> 00:02:35.213
Evolution predicts broad agreement among fossils, structures, and sequences.

36
00:02:36.693 --> 00:02:38.433
Their broad patterns largely agree.

37
00:02:39.467 --> 00:02:43.747
One limb or one small protein cannot settle every relationship.

38
00:02:45.447 --> 00:02:47.687
Similar jobs can evolve in different structures.

39
00:02:49.187 --> 00:02:53.447
Scientists compare more body parts, more sequences, and the fossil record.

40
00:02:54.517 --> 00:02:56.237
Return to the family tree.

41
00:02:57.757 --> 00:02:59.777
Matching parts show inherited construction.

42
00:03:01.097 --> 00:03:04.937
Similar sequences in the same protein add an independent check.

43
00:03:06.197 --> 00:03:10.617
When both fit one branch pattern, common ancestry explains the match best.

44
00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:13.540
The clues do different jobs.

45
00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:16.320
Fossils place life in time.

46
00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:22.080
Bones of living and fossil animals, plus sequences, test relationships.

47
00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:28.520
Their broad agreement supports evolution, while every uncertainty stays visible and

48
00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:29.380
open to testing.
